---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoToMp3 from '@/components/tools/media/VideoToMp3.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="video-to-mp3">
  <VideoToMp3 client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a video to MP3</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the video in. FFmpeg reads MP4, MOV, MKV, WebM, AVI and more.</li>
      <li>Pick a bitrate. 192 kbps is the default and sounds the same as the source for most music; 96 kbps is enough for speech.</li>
      <li>For only part of the soundtrack, turn on <strong>Trim before extracting</strong>, then drag the handles or type exact timecodes.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Extract MP3</strong>. The estimated size updates as you change the settings.</li>
      <li>Play the result in the built-in player and download it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Choosing a bitrate</h2>
    <p>
      A higher MP3 bitrate gives better fidelity and a larger file. The figures below are for stereo audio;
      mono halves them.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Bitrate</th>
            <th>Per minute</th>
            <th>One hour</th>
            <th>Sounds like</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>320 kbps</td>
            <td>2.4 MB</td>
            <td>144 MB</td>
            <td>The MP3 maximum; use it for music you will edit again</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>256 kbps</td>
            <td>1.9 MB</td>
            <td>115 MB</td>
            <td>Very good; the difference from 320 is hard to hear</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>192 kbps</td>
            <td>1.4 MB</td>
            <td>86 MB</td>
            <td>Recommended default for music</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>128 kbps</td>
            <td>0.9 MB</td>
            <td>58 MB</td>
            <td>Fine on phone speakers, thin on headphones</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>96 kbps</td>
            <td>0.7 MB</td>
            <td>43 MB</td>
            <td>Speech, lectures, podcasts</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Generation loss, and how to avoid it</h2>
    <p>
      The soundtrack in an MP4 is almost always AAC, which is already lossy. Re-encoding it to MP3 is a second
      lossy pass, so some detail is lost, mostly in cymbals, sibilance and room reverb. A higher bitrate
      limits the loss but cannot undo the first pass.
    </p>
    <p>
      To get the audio exactly as it is in the video, use
      <a href="/extract-audio-from-video">extract audio from video</a> in stream-copy mode. It copies the AAC
      track unchanged into an <code>.m4a</code> file. Convert to MP3 only when your workflow needs MP3.
    </p>

    <h2>Common uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Lectures and webinars: 96 kbps mono turns a 1 GB screen recording into a file you can listen to on a walk.</li>
      <li>Interviews for transcription: most transcription apps accept MP3 and charge by the minute, so trim first.</li>
      <li>Music from your own recordings: take the audio from a camera recording of a rehearsal.</li>
      <li>Voice notes: extract, trim to the useful part, and keep the file small enough to email.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The conversion runs in this tab with FFmpeg compiled to WebAssembly, so interviews, medical recordings and
      unreleased material stay on your computer. To confirm, go offline after the engine has loaded once; the
      tool still works.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
